<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empedocles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 494 – c. 434 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Empedocles was a Greek philosopher, poet and statesman from Acragas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In his poems he taught that all things are made of four roots, earth, water, air and fire, mixed and separated by the forces of Love and Strife, an idea that shaped the later theory of the four elements. He supported democracy in his city, and legend says that he died by leaping into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empedocles.jpg" alt="Empedocles" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empedokles.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
